            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not in favour of adding such a facility."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": ":-( the alternative is to bring in things like python3-pexpect which I was trying to avoid. One of the main usecases was to dump states of a target controller and hook that up into phosphor-debug-collector."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "@andrew@codeconstruct.com.au - Are there any alternative ideas for being able to programmatically interact with the obmc-console?  \"I'm not in favour\" doesn't give us much in terms of why and/or direction.\n\nI can say I'm not really liking the \"here is the console pattern to look at\" part of this.  Could we simplify it to be something like this?\n\n1) Get the most recent line (or partial line).\n2) Inject the following characters into the console."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "To be honest, we could live with just an inject and exit since the output would be in the logfile. Dont really need to wait on the term output. I was trying to make this look like `ssh root@<blah> cat /etc/os-release`."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> the alternative is to bring in things like python3-pexpect\n\nGoing from \"can I add 100 lines of C to obmc-console\" to \"we need to drag in the python ecosystem and pexpect\" is a huge leap. You've just demonstrated you can do the thing in 100 lines of C, why all of a sudden python?\n\nI don't see why you can't write an application that creates a pair of pipes, forks, and in the child, dup them to stdin/stdout then exec obmc-console-client to connect to the unix domain socket. Slightly more brittle would be composing the unix domain socket address for the server yourself and connecting directly (not having to fork/exec).\n\nEither way, from there, you do what you did with this patch, but in your own application.\n\nIf that's too raw, then there's always libexpect, or expect itself.\n\nA stock expect install on my debian system is ~5MB:\n\n```\n[I] 0 andrew@heihei ~> apt show expect tcl8.6 libtcl8.6 tcl-expect 2>/dev/null | grep Installed-Size | cut -d' ' -f2 | tr '\\n' '+' | tr -d ',' | sed -E 's/$/0/' | math\n5135\n```\n\nBy comparison for python appears to be ~14MB:\n\n```\n[I] 0 andrew@heihei ~> apt show python3.11 libpython3.11-stdlib libpython3.11-minimal 2>/dev/null | grep Installed-Size | cut -d' ' -f2 | tr '\\n' '+' | tr -d ',' | sed -E 's/$/0/' | math\n14531\n```\n\nI'd be surprised if the expect/tcl install couldn't be cut down further.\n\nI prefer we don't start influencing the data stream in obmc-console-client, beyond potentially handling escape sequences to issue e.g. a break on the UART. _That_ introspection is bounded to affecting behaviour of the hardware. I don't think obmc-console should implement interactions with the entity on the remote side - it should remain a transport mechanism."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the comment Andrew. let me think this through and get back. I was able to get some things done using the `expect` CLI."
                }
            ],
